# What a driving session leaves lying in the checkout. All of it is derived —
# `drive.mjs save` writes the whole book, `consistency` and `crops` write their
# findings — and all of it has a real home elsewhere: the book file belongs on
# the shelf under `books/<slug>/book.json`, and a findings file is read once and
# thrown away. Ignored rather than tidied by hand because `git add -A` is how a
# session commits, and `book.out.json` is two megabytes: it rode into this
# repository on one such commit and was re-committed on the four after it,
# where git now keeps every one of those versions for good. That is the same
# rule the scans note below states, learned again the expensive way.
book.out.json
consistency.json
findings.json

# Scans dropped into `public/` so the dev server can serve them — scratch for a
# driving session, never repository content. A scan belongs on the shelf under
# its own SHA-256, written once; git keeps every version of everything forever,
# so a 19 MB PDF committed here is 19 MB in the history for good. The generated
# fixtures are the exception: they are small, deterministic, and rebuilt by
# `scripts/make-test-*.mjs`.
public/*.pdf
public/*.epub
!public/test-*.pdf
!public/test-*.epub
